<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karađorđe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Karađorđe Petrović, Black George</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karađorđe was a livestock trader and former Austrian volunteer whom an assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orasac">Orašac</a> chose in February 1804 to lead the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-serbian-uprising">First Serbian Uprising</a> against the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>. He won major victories and ruled revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> as supreme leader until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> crushed the uprising in 1813. On his return in 1817 he was murdered on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>'s orders, founding the feud of two dynasties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karadorde.jpg" alt="Karađorđe" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Borovikovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kara%C4%91or%C4%91e_Petrovi%C4%87,_by_Vladimir_Borovikovsky,_1816.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Đorđe Petrović was born on 14 November 1768 in the village of Viševac in the Šumadija region of Ottoman Serbia, the eldest of five children in an impoverished family. His father had been a highwayman, or hajduk, in his youth and was by then a peasant farmer. Like most of his contemporaries Petrović was illiterate. He worked as a shepherd in adolescence and in 1785 married Jelena Jovanović; they had seven children, six of whom reached adulthood. In 1787 the family settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, fearing persecution by Ottoman janissaries.</p><p>After the Austro-Turkish War of 1788–1791 began he joined the Serbian Free Corps, a volunteer militia armed and trained by the Austrians, was decorated for bravery and rose to sergeant. The Treaty of Sistova of 1791 returned the captured territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and the rebels were crushed by 1792. He took refuge again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, then returned to Šumadija as a livestock merchant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In January and February 1804 renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> known as Dahis killed between 70 and 150 Serbian chieftains. Karađorđe, who narrowly escaped, was unanimously elected leader of the uprising by the surviving chiefs that February, and held the title of Grand Vožd from 14 February 1804 to 3 October 1813. Within six months most Dahi leaders were captured and executed. The rebels beat Ottoman regulars at Ivankovac in August 1805, and by 1806 held all the major towns of the Pashalik, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, and expelled their Muslim inhabitants. Muslims, combatants and non-combatants alike, were killed.</p><p>He was feared for a volatile temper and, according to the military historian Brendon A. Rehm, personally killed 125 people. In 1806 he ordered his own brother Marinko hanged. He refused the extensive autonomy offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-iii">Selim III</a> because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had promised to aid the rebels. Infighting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of Russia in 1812 weakened them, and he fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in October 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> arrested him and handed him over to the Russians, who gave him refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in secret in July 1817 and was killed shortly afterwards by agents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a>, who feared that his reappearance would make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> renege on concessions agreed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a> of 1815. The murder began a decades-long feud between the Karađorđević and Obrenović houses, ended in June 1903 when officers killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-obrenovic">Alexander Obrenović</a> and Karađorđe's grandson Peter Karađorđević took the throne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karađorđe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom grew out of the Principality of Serbia, created by the Serbian revolution of 1804–1817 under <b>Karađorđe</b> and then Miloš Obrenović, and ruled from 1817 by the Obrenović dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After they murdered dozens of Serbian notables, an assembly at Orašac in February 1804 chose <b>Karađorđe Petrović</b> to lead a revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Viševac, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in Radovanjski Lug, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.25,20.92&amp;year=1799" aria-label="Viševac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Karađorđe"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4446 3772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4446 3772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.25,20.92&amp;year=1799">What was here in 1799? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
